    "updated_at": "2021-09-21T18:36:02Z",
    "body": "`DOMException` has `name`, which behaves as a locale-neutral identifier (the fact that it's a string is fine--it just basically functions as an open-ended enum). I'm totally cool with `name` and think that specifications should define named exceptions. A named exception + data can be used to create localized messages on the client side with no fingerprinting and no need for language negotiation, etc. This is what I18N recommends to specifications. But...\r\n\r\nBut `DOMException` also has `message` which is \"just a string\" and is meant to be human-readable. I suppose localizing the message is a potential fingerprinting vector (to the degree that negotiating language/locale with anyone is a fingerprinting vector). Hardcoding all messages in English is not internationalized--I think our WG would prefer to provide for localization when it can be available?\r\n\r\nIIRC, DOMException's [ctor](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/DOMException/DOMException) encourages the creation of `message` by not providing a `name`-only ctor.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/webidl/issues/comments/924258922/reactions",
      "total_count": 0,
